<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Release-variant manifest overlay: the empty host Activity that Robolectric's
  `createComposeRule()` launches.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

  19 of app2's host-JVM unit-test files drive real compositions through
  `createComposeRule()`. That rule launches
  `androidx.activity.ComponentActivity` via `ActivityScenario`, so the activity
  must be resolvable in the merged manifest Robolectric is pointed at.

  For a LIBRARY module (`:shared:ui-kit`, `:shared:core-terminal`) AGP builds a
  dedicated unit-test manifest, `packaged_manifests/<variant>UnitTest/
  process<Variant>UnitTestManifest/AndroidManifest.xml`, and merges the
  `testImplementation` graph into it, so `ui-test-manifest` on the test
  configuration is enough there.

  An APPLICATION module gets NO such merge. `generate<Variant>UnitTestConfig`
  writes `android_merged_manifest=build/intermediates/packaged_manifests/
  <variant>/process<Variant>ManifestForPackage/AndroidManifest.xml`, the APP's
  own manifest, built from `src/main` plus the build-type source set plus the
  *main* variant's dependency graph. Nothing on `testImplementation` or
  `testReleaseImplementation` can reach it. So the ONLY way the release unit
  tests can resolve a host activity is for the release variant's own manifest
  to declare one.

  WHY NOT JUST `releaseImplementation(libs.compose.ui.test.manifest)`

  That AAR declares the activity with `android:exported="true"`, which would
  put an externally launchable empty activity into every shipped release APK.
  Declaring it here instead keeps it `android:exported="false"`: the app never
  starts it, no other app can, and it carries no intent filter, so it is inert
  outside the Robolectric host-JVM runtime. `androidx.activity.ComponentActivity`
  itself is already in the release APK, since `activity-compose` is an
  `implementation` dependency, so this adds a manifest entry, not code.

  WHY NOT DISABLE THE RELEASE UNIT-TEST VARIANT INSTEAD

  `androidComponents.beforeVariants { enableUnitTest = false }` would silence
  the failure by deleting the lane. The CI Unit lane is sharded by variant
  (issue #2069) precisely so "union of the shards == the whole test graph";
  dropping `:app2:testReleaseUnitTest` would break that invariant and force
  matching exceptions into scripts/check-executed-test-counts.sh and the #2082
  attendance selector, machinery whose whole job is to refuse exactly this kind
  of silently unrun module. Declaring the activity keeps the invariant
  mechanical and the shard honest.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application>
        <activity
            android:name="androidx.activity.ComponentActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar" />
    </application>

</manifest>
